WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.171 --> 00:00:05.054
<v Maya>You know, for a long time, we've kind of treated the labor market like a like a medieval map.</v>

2
00:00:05.374 --> 00:00:07.695
<v Daniel>Oh, yeah. With little sea monsters on the edges.</v>

3
00:00:07.815 --> 00:00:16.220
<v Maya>Exactly. Like we drew these very firm, very comforting boundaries around human work. On one side, you had the routine, repetitive jobs.</v>

4
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:18.408
<v Daniel>Right. The data entry, the assembly lines.</v>

5
00:00:18.609 --> 00:00:27.395
<v Maya>Yeah. And we basically accepted that machines would take those over eventually. But on the other side of that line, we essentially wrote, you know, here be humans.</v>

6
00:00:27.555 --> 00:00:27.715
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

7
00:00:28.116 --> 00:00:33.200
<v Maya>Those were the safe zones, the jobs requiring real creativity, nuance, complex thought.</v>

8
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:45.272
<v Daniel>Well, and it was a very clean way of looking at the world. I mean, honestly, a deeply reassuring one. We really believed our human intuition and our cultural understanding were just exclusive territories that silicon just couldn't conquer.</v>

9
00:00:45.432 --> 00:00:53.562
<v Maya>Right. But then you start tracking the actual data over the last decade or so, and suddenly you realize that boundary line, well, it isn't drawn in permanent marker.</v>

10
00:00:54.062 --> 00:00:54.683
<v Daniel>No, not at all.</v>

11
00:00:54.923 --> 00:01:04.571
<v Maya>And a brand new paper published on October 14th, 2026 from NCA researchers. It finally gives us the hard data to prove that the line is moving and it is moving fast. Yeah,</v>

12
00:01:04.591 --> 00:01:05.051
<v Daniel>it really is.</v>

13
00:01:05.352 --> 00:01:15.560
<v Maya>Which forces us to ask an incredibly uncomfortable question for anyone listening today. Are we simply redrawing the map of human work or is the very concept of a safe zone fundamentally flawed?</v>

14
00:01:15.927 --> 00:01:28.790
<v Daniel>Yeah, and that is the core of our mission today. We are looking at a really profound question about the future of our careers. Basically, do the tasks that resisted one generation of AI keep resisting the next?</v>

15
00:01:29.331 --> 00:01:43.935
<v Maya>Right. Welcome to the Deep Dive. To figure this out, we're looking at a pretty fascinating stack of research, but the absolute star of the show today is this new 2026 paper in management science from Erdem Dogukan Yilmaz, Ivana Naumovska, and</v>

16
00:01:44.055 --> 00:01:45.335
<v Maya>Vikas A. Agarwal.</v>

17
00:01:45.745 --> 00:01:46.445
<v Daniel>The INSEAD team.</v>

18
00:01:46.986 --> 00:02:01.732
<v Maya>Exactly. And what they've done is isolate exactly how AI is substituting human labor in real time. OK, let's unpack this, because to truly grasp the shock of their findings, we actually have to look at the old rules of automation first. The rules that managers and economists have relied on for</v>

19
00:02:01.772 --> 00:02:02.192
<v Maya>decades.</v>

20
00:02:02.913 --> 00:02:09.956
<v Daniel>Yeah. And the foundation of those rules really goes back to a classic 2003 paper by economists Otter, Levy and Murnane.</v>

21
00:02:10.282 --> 00:02:12.404
<v Maya>Right. The routine versus non-routine framework.</v>

22
00:02:12.704 --> 00:02:24.896
<v Daniel>Exactly. They established this framework that the whole field still relies on today. And their core finding was that computers substituted for routine tasks, but they actually complemented non-routine cognitive work.</v>

23
00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:31.662
<v Maya>So if you were doing like complex analysis or problem solving, the early assumption was that the computer didn't replace you at all.</v>

24
00:02:32.142 --> 00:02:35.025
<v Daniel>Right. It just made you a faster, more valuable human.</v>

25
00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:45.711
<v Maya>Yeah. And language translation has historically been the ultimate example of a non-routine cognitive task. I mean, it requires shifting contexts, understanding idioms, making judgment calls.</v>

26
00:02:46.671 --> 00:02:55.594
<v Daniel>And then researchers Carl Benedict Frey and Michael Osborne built heavily on that back in 2017. They defined what they called the bottlenecks of automation.</v>

27
00:02:56.054 --> 00:02:57.074
<v Maya>The bottlenecks. Yeah.</v>

28
00:02:58.018 --> 00:03:09.808
<v Daniel>They argued that computers would severely struggle with anything requiring genuine creative intelligence or social intelligence. Their firm belief was that machines just couldn't handle nuance or cultural context.</v>

29
00:03:10.148 --> 00:03:24.878
<v Maya>Which brings us to a massive reality check, because we have a 2025 follow-up paper by Frey and Lanzos-Paredes. They looked at 695 local labor markets in the US, and they found that the adoption of Google Translate</v>

30
00:03:25.058 --> 00:03:37.626
<v Maya>alone resulted in roughly 28,000 fewer translator positions between 2010 and 2023. It even reduced the overall demand for foreign language skills in the broader job market.</v>

31
00:03:38.208 --> 00:03:52.539
<v Daniel>I mean, that is a staggering aggregate number. It proves that predictive machine translation had a very real, very negative impact on human employment in a field that was supposed to be completely non-routine and protected.</v>

32
00:03:53.165 --> 00:04:02.075
<v Maya>But, you know, if you look at the wider gig economy, things don't add up perfectly. Freelancers in general haven't lost jobs at anywhere near that scale.</v>

33
00:04:02.155 --> 00:04:02.876
<v Daniel>Right. They really haven't.</v>

34
00:04:03.237 --> 00:04:18.053
<v Maya>So why did translators get hit so hard? And more importantly, are all translators losing jobs or just some? That's the real question. Because it's like, think of it like looking at a forest after a storm from a helicopter. You see 28,000</v>

35
00:04:18.093 --> 00:04:25.641
<v Maya>trees down, but you don't know which trees fell. Did the storm take down the deep-rooted oaks, or did it just snap off the fragile branches?</v>

36
00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:41.283
<v Daniel>What's fascinating here is that helicopter view is actually the problem with aggregate data. It completely hides the task level mechanism. It shows us the shrinking forest, sure, but it doesn't show us the individual branches. To see exactly what is happening on the ground,</v>

37
00:04:41.303 --> 00:04:44.286
<v Daniel>we need to look at the task level data in this new 2026 INSEAD paper.</v>

38
00:04:45.663 --> 00:05:00.176
<v Maya>And the INSEAD authors found a brilliant way to isolate that mechanism. They studied two sequential natural experiments within one of the world's largest online labor markets for freelance work. And the first natural experiment happens in late 2016</v>

39
00:05:00.556 --> 00:05:04.960
<v Maya>with the introduction of GNMT, which is Google Neural Machine Translation.</v>

40
00:05:05.387 --> 00:05:20.217
<v Daniel>Yeah. And we should clarify what GNMT actually is. It's what we call a predictive task-specific AI. Google rolled it out as a really quiet backend upgrade that replaced their old phrase-based translation engine. And it was a massive</v>

41
00:05:20.257 --> 00:05:27.462
<v Daniel>leap in quality because instead of translating word by word, it used neural networks to predict the most accurate sequence for a whole sentence.</v>

42
00:05:28.083 --> 00:05:32.726
<v Maya>Oh, I remember when that happened. Google Translate suddenly stopped sounding like a robot.</v>

43
00:05:32.966 --> 00:05:41.627
<v Daniel>Exactly. But architecturally, it was still a highly specialized tool. It was designed for one convergent task, which was literal accuracy.</v>

44
00:05:42.293 --> 00:05:50.235
<v Maya>Which brings up a crucial distinction in the paper that we really need to define for everyone. The researchers divide translation into two entirely different types of work.</v>

45
00:05:50.395 --> 00:05:50.575
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

46
00:05:51.275 --> 00:06:04.079
<v Maya>Regular translation versus transcreation. So regular translation is basically analytical, almost like math, just swapping vocabulary with strict fidelity to the original text, like translating a car manual.</v>

47
00:06:04.519 --> 00:06:06.240
<v Daniel>Or a standard news report, yeah.</v>

48
00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.181
<v Maya>But transcreation, I mean, that sounds like you have to actually be a local to do it.</v>

49
00:06:11.557 --> 00:06:21.426
<v Daniel>That is the perfect way to frame it. Transcreation is the difference between science and art. It is intuitive. It's divergent and highly creative.</v>

50
00:06:22.066 --> 00:06:22.447
<v Maya>Like what?</v>

51
00:06:23.348 --> 00:06:33.857
<v Daniel>Think about translating poetic advertising copy or a cultural joke or a literary novel. You aren't just converting words. You are performing a cultural adaptation.</v>

52
00:06:35.208 --> 00:06:39.791
<v Maya>Oh, OK. So you have to know what makes a French teenager laugh versus an American teenager.</v>

53
00:06:40.111 --> 00:06:40.491
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

54
00:06:40.731 --> 00:06:46.335
<v Maya>Like translating a pun so it actually lands rather than just sounding like literal translated nonsense.</v>

55
00:06:46.555 --> 00:06:56.101
<v Daniel>Yeah. And you are judged purely on the emotional effect it has on the new audience, not literal fidelity. It requires a deep lived understanding of human culture.</v>

56
00:06:56.801 --> 00:07:09.156
<v Maya>So to measure how that 2016 Predictive AI impacted these two different types of tasks. The INSEAD researchers used this fascinating study design because GNMT didn't launch for every language all at once.</v>

57
00:07:09.736 --> 00:07:11.257
<v Daniel>Right. It had a staggered rollout.</v>

58
00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:18.400
<v Maya>Yeah. It hit eight language pairs in November 2016 and then 33 more pairs in March and April of 2017.</v>

59
00:07:18.821 --> 00:07:20.101
<v Daniel>Which is great for researchers.</v>

60
00:07:20.401 --> 00:07:30.607
<v Maya>It's amazing. It's like testing a new medicine by giving it to half a classroom in March and the other half in April. You can watch exactly when the sneezing stops to prove the medicine actually works.</v>

61
00:07:31.058 --> 00:07:44.103
<v Daniel>Right. That staggered rollout allows for what economists call a difference in differences design. By comparing the language pairs that got the AI upgrade against the ones that hadn't gotten it yet, they could isolate the exact impact of the technology.</v>

62
00:07:44.884 --> 00:07:49.766
<v Maya>And they looked at a lot of data from January 2016 to May 2017 across over 38,000 transactions.</v>

63
00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:03.023
<v Daniel>And the headline numbers for this first experiment are incredibly telling. GNMT cut the demand for regular translation by about 9% within just seven months.</v>

64
00:08:03.703 --> 00:08:05.764
<v Maya>Wow. So it took a real bite out of the analytical work.</v>

65
00:08:06.384 --> 00:08:09.145
<v Daniel>It did. But as for transcreation.</v>

66
00:08:10.025 --> 00:08:10.245
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

67
00:08:10.846 --> 00:08:13.927
<v Daniel>It had absolutely no detectable effect. Zero.</v>

68
00:08:14.227 --> 00:08:14.807
<v Maya>Wait, really?</v>

69
00:08:15.227 --> 00:08:15.487
<v Daniel>None.</v>

70
00:08:15.727 --> 00:08:16.227
<v Maya>None at all.</v>

71
00:08:16.547 --> 00:08:27.491
<v Daniel>The transactions and the revenue for intuitive creative translation stayed essentially flat. So that 2017 creative bottleneck defined by Frayne Osborne, it held perfectly firm.</v>

72
00:08:28.051 --> 00:08:36.297
<v Maya>And if you're listening to this and you're in a highly specialized technical field, say medical coding or legal analysis, this is the moment your ears should prick up.</v>

73
00:08:36.637 --> 00:08:37.158
<v Daniel>Oh, definitely.</v>

74
00:08:37.458 --> 00:08:47.385
<v Maya>Because GNMT did absolutely nothing to technical translation either. Things like highly specialized legal, medical, or engineering documents were completely untouched.</v>

75
00:08:47.873 --> 00:08:59.833
<v Daniel>Right. The authors call this the knowledge barrier. Predictive AI stayed firmly in its analytical, general knowledge lane. It couldn't leap the hurdle of specialized jargon, and it couldn't replicate the spark of human creativity.</v>

76
00:09:00.375 --> 00:09:05.497
<v Maya>Which means Generation 1 of AI basically respected the boundaries we drew on that medieval map.</v>

77
00:09:05.957 --> 00:09:06.237
<v Daniel>It did.</v>

78
00:09:06.577 --> 00:09:16.220
<v Maya>Here's where it gets really interesting. Because the second natural experiment in the paper throws those rules entirely out the window. We move from late 2016 to November 2022. The launch of ChatGPT.</v>

79
00:09:19.861 --> 00:09:32.274
<v Daniel>Right. And we are dealing with a monumental shift in architecture here. Unlike GNMT, which was a task specific predictive model, ChatGPT is a broad general purpose generative AI.</v>

80
00:09:32.914 --> 00:09:41.423
<v Maya>But how does a general purpose AI suddenly understand a French pun better than a dedicated translation AI? Like what's the actual mechanism there?</v>

81
00:09:41.975 --> 00:09:50.439
<v Daniel>It really comes down to the training data and the goal of the model. A translation AI is trained to find the most statistically probable target word, period.</v>

82
00:09:50.579 --> 00:09:50.759
<v Maya>Right.</v>

83
00:09:51.179 --> 00:10:02.104
<v Daniel>But a large language model like ChatGPT is trained on a vast, messy corpus of human existence. Reddit threads, Wikipedia debates, classic literature, bad jokes.</v>

84
00:10:02.405 --> 00:10:03.125
<v Maya>Just all of it.</v>

85
00:10:03.425 --> 00:10:17.856
<v Daniel>Exactly. By digesting all of that context, it learns the underlying patterns of human psychology and culture. It isn't just predicting a sequence of translated words. It's synthesizing concepts. It essentially approximates human intuition.</v>

86
00:10:18.276 --> 00:10:30.785
<v Maya>That's wild. But measuring its impact on the freelance market must be a methodological nightmare. Because unlike Google's staggered rollout, ChatGPT hit every language pair globally on the exact same day.</v>

87
00:10:31.126 --> 00:10:32.727
<v Daniel>Right. It was everywhere once.</v>

88
00:10:33.109 --> 00:10:40.215
<v Maya>So there is no half classroom to test the medicine on. How did the authors study a shock that happened everywhere simultaneously?</v>

89
00:10:40.555 --> 00:10:47.361
<v Daniel>Well, since they couldn't compare different languages over time, they defined the treatment by looking at task composition instead.</v>

90
00:10:48.102 --> 00:10:48.882
<v Maya>Okay, how does that work?</v>

91
00:10:49.463 --> 00:11:00.472
<v Daniel>They compared pure translation jobs, where the only task is translating text, against what they call bundled jobs. Bundled jobs are projects where translation is just one component of a larger workflow.</v>

92
00:11:01.171 --> 00:11:12.075
<v Maya>Oh, right. Because you can ask an AI to translate a YouTube script, but you can't easily ask it to, you know, record the human voiceover or conduct original field research or manage a physical event.</v>

93
00:11:12.275 --> 00:11:23.620
<v Daniel>Precisely. So they looked at over 82,000 transactions from January 2022 to May 2023. And the authors actually call this a conservative control group.</v>

94
00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:24.700
<v Maya>Why conservative?</v>

95
00:11:25.108 --> 00:11:34.915
<v Daniel>Because obviously those bundle jobs were partially exposed to the AI shock as well. Like if the AI speeds up the translation part of the bundle, the overall price of the bundle might drop too.</v>

96
00:11:35.367 --> 00:11:47.814
<v Maya>Okay, but even with a conservative control, the numbers they found are an absolute landslide. Remember how GNMT cut regular translation by 9%? Yeah. ChatGPT cut regular translation by about 21%.</v>

97
00:11:48.095 --> 00:11:48.735
<v Daniel>Huge jump.</v>

98
00:11:48.935 --> 00:11:58.681
<v Maya>Huge. But the real shocker is transcreation. ChatGPT didn't respect the creative bottleneck at all. It cut demand for transcreation by about 30%.</v>

99
00:11:58.881 --> 00:11:59.802
<v Daniel>It's unbelievable.</v>

100
00:12:00.202 --> 00:12:03.604
<v Maya>And total revenue across the board fell by roughly a quarter.</v>

101
00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:14.650
<v Daniel>It is the total collapse of the boundary, the protective wall between analytical and creative work that held up during the predictive AI era. It completely disintegrated under generative AI.</v>

102
00:12:15.151 --> 00:12:16.212
<v Maya>And that knowledge barrier.</v>

103
00:12:16.452 --> 00:12:20.236
<v Daniel>The highly technical medical and legal documents that were supposed to be completely safe.</v>

104
00:12:20.696 --> 00:12:21.357
<v Maya>Obliterated.</v>

105
00:12:21.737 --> 00:12:23.839
<v Daniel>Demand for technical translation dropped by about 26%.</v>

106
00:12:25.268 --> 00:12:33.109
<v Maya>To put the sheer scale of this into perspective, we have to look at one more piece of the puzzle. There is a 2024 paper from Hui, Reshef, and Zhou.</v>

107
00:12:33.289 --> 00:12:35.530
<v Daniel>Right, the one that looked at the overall freelance market?</v>

108
00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:50.292
<v Maya>Yeah, exactly, after ChatGPT launched. They found that across all exposed occupations, freelancers lost about 2% of their monthly jobs and about 5.2% of earnings, just 2% of jobs overall. Yeah. So you have a 2% drop in the general gig</v>

109
00:12:50.332 --> 00:13:04.225
<v Maya>economy, but a 30% drop in transcreation tasks. Which forces us to ask, does this 30% drop mean that ChatGPT is actually writing better poetry or translating ad copy with more cultural nuance than human experts?</v>

110
00:13:04.606 --> 00:13:12.632
<v Daniel>That is the most crucial distinction we have to make today. If we connect this to the bigger picture, the answer is no. The AI doesn't have to be better.</v>

111
00:13:13.193 --> 00:13:13.493
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

112
00:13:13.693 --> 00:13:25.899
<v Daniel>The authors emphasize that a system does not need to perfectly match human cultural judgment to take the job. The bar for market substitution is much, much lower than the bar for parity on a scientific benchmark.</v>

113
00:13:26.499 --> 00:13:32.821
<v Maya>Because good enough is the enemy of perfect, especially when good enough is practically free and instant.</v>

114
00:13:33.201 --> 00:13:48.145
<v Daniel>Exactly. Think about a mid-sized company trying to localize an ad campaign. If a generative AI can get a transcreation task 85% of the way there in three seconds for fractions of a penny, the market will substitute it for human labor.</v>

115
00:13:48.798 --> 00:13:52.160
<v Maya>Even if it misses a subtle emotional cue that a human master would have caught.</v>

116
00:13:52.480 --> 00:14:01.785
<v Daniel>Exactly. And we also have to remember that ChatGPT's massive numbers represent the marginal effect on a market that had already been shaped by six years of predictive AI.</v>

117
00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:16.942
<v Maya>Oh, right. So GNMT had already skimmed the easy analytical translation work off the top back in 2017. ChatGPT came in and took huge bites out of the complex intuitive work that was left over. Now, I know some people</v>

118
00:14:16.982 --> 00:14:24.468
<v Maya>listening might be skeptical. They might be thinking, are we sure this isn't just a statistical fluke? Like maybe the freelance market just slowed down in 2023.</v>

119
00:14:25.542 --> 00:14:35.082
<v Daniel>The authors anticipated that exact skepticism, and they went to incredible lengths to prove the data is rock solid. They ran rigorous robustness checks.</v>

120
00:14:35.649 --> 00:14:44.695
<v Maya>They stress tested this data every way possible. Instead of boring you with terms like Poisson's pseudo-maximum likelihood models and coarsened exact matching.</v>

121
00:14:44.915 --> 00:14:45.636
<v Daniel>We love those terms.</v>

122
00:14:45.756 --> 00:14:56.143
<v Maya>We do. But let's look at what they actually did. First, they ran fake timeline tests, placebo dates, to make sure the job losses didn't magically start before the AI was actually launched.</v>

123
00:14:56.463 --> 00:14:57.464
<v Daniel>And they didn't. Right.</v>

124
00:14:58.404 --> 00:15:03.768
<v Maya>Then they mathematically matched similar freelancers to each other to ensure they were comparing apples to apples.</v>

125
00:15:04.416 --> 00:15:17.542
<v Daniel>They even brought in a different AI to check their own human work. Right. They used a BERT language model to independently classify the thousands of job postings into regular versus transcreation just to ensure their manual coding was accurate.</v>

126
00:15:17.622 --> 00:15:23.245
<v Maya>Which is deeply ironic. Using an AI to classify tasks to prove another AI took human jobs.</v>

127
00:15:23.305 --> 00:15:23.805
<v Daniel>It really is.</v>

128
00:15:24.245 --> 00:15:39.082
<v Maya>They also looked at individual freelancer panels, which showed that established veteran freelancers saw about a 9% drop in their personal job volume. They even controlled for a baseline period from 2018 to 2019 and accounted for Microsoft launching its own neural translation</v>

129
00:15:39.142 --> 00:15:40.424
<v Maya>engine back in 2016.</v>

130
00:15:40.784 --> 00:15:41.725
<v Daniel>So the data is bulletproof.</v>

131
00:15:41.966 --> 00:15:42.867
<v Maya>Yeah, it really is.</v>

132
00:15:43.578 --> 00:15:55.380
<v Daniel>It is incredibly robust. But as with any scientific study, we have to honestly state the limitations of what this tells us. I mean, this research looks at one specific platform and one specific occupation.</v>

133
00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:56.100
<v Maya>Right.</v>

134
00:15:56.760 --> 00:16:03.542
<v Daniel>It's tracking freelancers, not full time salaried employees inside a corporation who might have different contract protections or severance structures.</v>

135
00:16:04.002 --> 00:16:11.863
<v Maya>And it's only measuring the short and medium term effects. We're tracking the immediate shockwaves, but we don't know what this ecosystem looks like a decade from now.</v>

136
00:16:12.341 --> 00:16:26.827
<v Daniel>Furthermore, we have to acknowledge that the two shocks happened in entirely different macroeconomic environments. I mean, the global economy of 2016 is not the economy of late 2022. Right. And there is a massive hype factor to consider. ChatGPT received</v>

137
00:16:26.987 --> 00:16:35.130
<v Daniel>unprecedented global publicity from day one. GNMT was a quiet backend upgrade that most users didn't even realize had happened.</v>

138
00:16:35.613 --> 00:16:42.516
<v Maya>Oh, that's a great point. That massive awareness undoubtedly drove faster, more aggressive market substitution for chat GPT.</v>

139
00:16:42.776 --> 00:16:43.217
<v Daniel>Absolutely.</v>

140
00:16:43.837 --> 00:16:58.664
<v Maya>So what does this all mean? We've gone from a world where computers only did routine data entry to an AI that can approximate the nuances of poetry fast enough to wipe out a third of the freelance market in months. What is the ultimate takeaway for the professionals,</v>

141
00:16:58.844 --> 00:17:01.265
<v Maya>the managers, and the students out there listening to this?</v>

142
00:17:01.906 --> 00:17:12.932
<v Daniel>The primary takeaway is about anticipating the nature of change. For years, the comforting career advice was always reskill into creative, intuitive, or highly technical roles to hedge against AI.</v>

143
00:17:13.092 --> 00:17:15.453
<v Maya>Yeah, that was the standard advice.</v>

144
00:17:15.953 --> 00:17:29.320
<v Daniel>This paper proves that strategy is no longer a reliable safety net. If the automation boundary moved once, from predictive AI respecting creativity to generative AI consuming it, you must expect the boundary to move again.</v>

145
00:17:30.158 --> 00:17:33.321
<v Maya>Which means the protective wall wasn't a wall at all. It was just a speed bump.</v>

146
00:17:33.621 --> 00:17:48.112
<v Daniel>Exactly. And for the translation industry specifically, it points to an inevitable structural shift. The future of human work in this space isn't raw from scratch translation. The market is moving toward post-editing, quality control, and AI</v>

147
00:17:48.472 --> 00:17:49.233
<v Daniel>supervision roles.</v>

148
00:17:49.633 --> 00:17:49.893
<v Maya>Right.</v>

149
00:17:50.394 --> 00:17:58.420
<v Daniel>Humans will essentially become the final arbiters of cultural taste, managing the output of the machines rather than generating the first draft themselves.</v>

150
00:17:58.833 --> 00:18:06.460
<v Maya>Before we wrap up, I want to leave you with one final deeply provocative thought that the authors themselves pose as an open question at the very end of their paper.</v>

151
00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:07.300
<v Daniel>Well, this is a big one.</v>

152
00:18:07.661 --> 00:18:22.484
<v Maya>It is. If generative AI can instantly and near perfectly translate any language, including all the creative and technical nuances, does this permanently weaken the human incentive to learn foreign languages in the first place? It's a heavy question. And ultimately, if we</v>

153
00:18:22.524 --> 00:18:32.197
<v Maya>don't need a common bridge language to communicate across borders anymore, could seamless AI translation actually mark the beginning of the end for the global dominance of English?</v>

154
00:18:32.922 --> 00:18:47.772
